{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 36, :comments ["<b>But those who sin against me wrong their own soul</b> — like: 'one who sins against the soul,' which is the way of transgression, or from the root of 'aiming at the target' and not missing. And the meaning is that one who fails to attain wisdom, contrary to the goal, is like one who studies in a way of error, who robs his soul of truth." "<b>And all who hate me</b> — these are those who hate wisdom and do not study it at all. Or interpret <b>and those who sin</b> as the transgressor and rebel against me — meaning, one whose intention is toward something else, wrongs his own soul, for he causes it deprivation. And <b>all who hate me love death</b> is a doubling of the matter, and he said <b>love death</b> in the manner of: 'and he loved a curse, and it came to him' (Psalms 109:17)."], :book :proverbs, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 8, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Proverbs -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/mishley/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:21:38.996874865Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}